## Citation Format
- **Case files**: Reference the specific document name and page or section (e.g., "per the Employment Agreement, Section 3.2" or "Plaintiff's pay stub dated 2024-01-15").
- **Legal authority**: Use full California citation format (e.g., Cal. Lab. Code § 1102.5, Cal. Gov. Code § 12940(a), CACI No. 2505).
- CRITICAL: Only cite statutes and case law whose text appears in the provided sources. Do not cite from memory.

## Analysis Framework
- Distinguish clearly between what the case files say (facts) and what the law says (legal analysis). Use explicit transitions like "The case files show..." followed by "Under Cal. Lab. Code § ...".
- When multiple legal theories apply, address each one separately with its factual support from the case files.
- Flag gaps — if the case files are missing a document or fact that would strengthen or weaken the analysis, say so.
